1. INTRODUCTION: THE FINAL FRONTIER
Aerospace is the most demanding certification domain on Earth.
DO-178C (Software Considerations in Airborne Systems and Equipment Certification)
defines the standard for software used in civil aviation. Its companion, DO-254 (Design
Assurance for Airborne Electronic Hardware), addresses hardware.
The challenge: AI in avionics has been treated as a "special case" — something that cannot
be certified under existing rules. The industry has been waiting for a new standard (or a
massive amendment) to allow stochastic neural networks into the flight deck.
ST-T1024 rejects this premise.
If an architecture is deterministic, auditable, and physically bounded, it fits DO-178C as
written. No amendments. No exceptions. No probabilistic "confidence scores."
---
2. DO-178C: OVERVIEW AND OBJECTIVES
DO-178C defines five Design Assurance Levels (DAL), from A (most critical) to E (least
critical):
DAL Failure Condition ST-T1024 Readiness
A Catastrophic ⚠️ Achievable with formal verification + triple-redundant FSM
B Hazardous/Severe ✅ Achievable with formal verification + FMEDA
C Major ✅ Achievable with FMEDA + standard testing
D Minor ✅ Exceeded — deterministic execution
E No effect ✅ Exceeded — baseline architecture
The core DO-178C objectives:

1. Requirements — System requirements are complete, correct, and traceable.
2. Design — Software architecture is deterministic and auditable.
3. Code — Implementation matches design, no unintended behavior.
4. Verification — Testing proves compliance.
5. Configuration Management — Changes are controlled and traceable.
